Z={
1:"Commentators note kāryaṃ karma, “work that should be done”: the true renunciant still does his duty. Madhusūdana says giving up the fire without giving up desire is only a change of clothes. Many teachers say the test of renunciation is inner freedom, visible in calm and kindness.",
2:"Commentators note that sannyāsa and yoga are called one because both require letting go of selfish plans. Madhusūdana says the karma yogi who drops saṅkalpa is a renunciant in spirit. Modern mindfulness teachers note that much anxiety comes from constant planning; letting it rest brings peace.",
3:"Commentators note kāraṇa, “means”: action is the means for the climber, calm for the one who has arrived. Madhusūdana says one should not mistake the stage. Many traditions use the image of a boat: you need it to cross the river, but not once you have landed.",
4:"Commentators note anuṣajjate, “clings”: the mark of arrival is no clinging to objects or to activity. Madhusūdana says this is a high state, reached after long practice. Teachers warn students not to claim it too early.",
5:"Commentators note avasādayet, “let sink”: the self can drag itself down through laziness, despair and wrong habits. Madhusūdana says no one else can save us if we refuse to help ourselves. Many modern self-help ideas, from self-efficacy to positive self-talk, echo this ancient verse.",
6:"Commentators note jitaḥ, “conquered”: the mind once mastered becomes an ally. Madhusūdana says the unconquered mind leads us into harm like an enemy who lives in our own house. Training the mind is the most important work, because the mind is with us everywhere.",
7:"Commentators note praśānta, “deeply peaceful”: peace comes after self-conquest. Madhusūdana says such a person is not shaken by heat or cold, praise or blame. Many saints met insults calmly; Jesus’ teaching to turn the other cheek is a familiar parallel.",
8:"Commentators note jñāna-vijñāna-tṛptātmā, “satisfied with knowledge and realisation”: the yogi’s contentment comes from understanding. Madhusūdana says one who has knowledge and its experience needs nothing more. The famous image of gold and clay shows freedom from greed, not ignorance of value.",
9:"Commentators note sādhuṣv api ca pāpeṣu, “among the good and the wicked”: even toward wrongdoers, the yogi holds no hatred. Madhusūdana says this is the highest test. Mahatma Gandhi often said, “hate the sin, not the sinner”, a principle close to this verse.",
10:"Commentators note rahasi, “in secret” or “in a secluded place”: meditation needs a protected space. Madhusūdana says solitude protects the beginner from distraction. Many homes in India have a small prayer room or corner, a daily place of quiet.",
11:"Commentators note śuci, “clean”: the place must be clean. Madhusūdana says a clean place helps a clean mind. Many meditation teachers today advise a tidy, uncluttered space, not for ritual reasons but because the outer environment affects the inner.",
12:"Commentators note upaviśya, “sitting”: meditation in the Gītā is done seated. Madhusūdana says purity of mind is the purpose, not special experiences. Many find that even ten minutes of sitting daily brings noticeable calm over weeks.",
13:"Commentators note acala, “motionless”: stillness of the body helps stillness of the mind. Madhusūdana says the spine should be straight so the breath moves freely. Modern teachers stress comfort too: a still body comes more easily from a relaxed posture than a forced one.",
14:"Commentators note vigata-bhīḥ, “free from fear”: fear makes meditation impossible. Madhusūdana says trust in the Lord removes fear. Many beginners feel restless or anxious when they first sit still; teachers advise patience and gentleness.",
15:"Commentators note niyata-mānasaḥ, “of controlled mind”: regular practice brings control. Madhusūdana says the peace that follows rests in the Lord. Many teachers say regularity matters more than duration: a short daily sitting does more than a long occasional one.",
16:"Commentators note atyaśnataḥ, “one who eats too much”, and anaśnataḥ, “one who does not eat”: both extremes disturb meditation. Madhusūdana says the right amount varies by person. Āyurveda advises eating only when hungry and stopping before full.",
17:"Commentators note yukta-ceṣṭa, “moderate in effort”: even effort must be balanced. Madhusūdana says too much effort strains, too little stagnates. Many sports and music teachers give the same advice: steady practice, not bursts.",
18:"Commentators note niḥspṛhaḥ sarva-kāmebhyaḥ, “free of longing for all desires”: the mind is settled because it wants nothing. Madhusūdana says this is the mark of real meditation. In practice, many meditators report moments when wanting simply stops.",
19:"Commentators note yata-cittasya, “of controlled mind”: the steady flame is the yogi’s mind. Madhusūdana says the windless place is the absence of desire. The Yoga Vāsiṣṭha, a later text, uses similar images of stillness, comparing the settled mind to a calm ocean without waves.",
20:"Commentators note uparamate, “comes to rest”: the mind stops of its own accord. Madhusūdana says this is not forced suppression but natural settling. The difference between forcing stillness and allowing it is one of the most important lessons in meditation.",
21:"Commentators note na calati tattvataḥ, “does not move from the truth”: once known, the inner joy anchors one. Madhusūdana says the experience is self-confirming. Many describe meditation’s rewards not as excitement but as a quiet, steady contentment.",
22:"Commentators note yaṃ labdhvā, “having gained which”: a gain greater than any other. Madhusūdana says even great sorrow cannot shake one established here. Many people who have faced terrible loss with calm describe an inner ground that held them.",
23:"Commentators note niścayena, “with determination”: yoga needs firm resolve. Madhusūdana says the yogi must persist even when progress seems slow. The verse is often quoted to students who feel discouraged.",
24:"Commentators note sarvān aśeṣataḥ, “all, without remainder”: desires born of imagination are to be given up completely. Madhusūdana says the senses are to be gathered from every side. The image is of a herdsman bringing all the cattle home in the evening.",
25:"Commentators note dhṛti-gṛhītayā, “held by firmness”: understanding must be steadied by will. Madhusūdana says patience is essential. Many teachers say meditation is like learning a musical instrument: daily practice, slow progress, sure results.",
26:"Commentators note cañcalam asthiram, “restless and unsteady”: the mind’s nature. Madhusūdana says one must not be angry with it, but bring it back gently. Some teachers compare the mind to a puppy being trained: patient, repeated, kind correction.",
27:"Commentators note praśānta-manasam, “of peaceful mind”: peace comes before supreme joy. Madhusūdana says joy arises naturally when restlessness ends. The verse shows that the path of meditation is not grim but leads to happiness.",
28:"Commentators note vigata-kalmaṣaḥ, “free of stain”: practice purifies. Madhusūdana says contact with Brahman brings joy without end. The phrase sukhena, “easily”, encourages: what began with effort ends in ease.",
29:"Commentators note sarvatra sama-darśanaḥ, “seeing the same everywhere”: the fruit of meditation is a new way of seeing. Madhusūdana says the meditator sees the Self in all and all in the Self. The vision naturally produces kindness.",
30:"Commentators note praṇaśyati, “is lost”: the Lord and devotee never lose sight of each other. Madhusūdana, though an Advaitin, was a devoted Kṛṣṇa-bhakta and loved this verse. Many devotees recite it in times of loneliness.",
31:"Commentators note ekatvam āsthitaḥ, “established in oneness”: the yogi sees the One in all. Madhusūdana says such a person lives in the Lord whatever his way of life. Monk or householder, rich or poor, the inner state is what matters.",
32:"Commentators note paramaḥ, “the highest”: the highest yogi is defined by empathy. Madhusūdana says feeling others’ joy and pain as one’s own is the fruit of seeing the Self in all. Modern research on compassion shows that empathy, rightly guided, increases wellbeing.",
33:"Commentators note sāmyena, “as evenness”: Arjuna recognises the core of the teaching. Madhusūdana says his doubt is about practice, not theory. Every honest seeker reaches this point: I understand, but can I do it?",
34:"Commentators note suduṣkaram, “very hard”: Arjuna does not say impossible. Madhusūdana says the mind is like wind, but wind can be harnessed. Sailors use the wind to move their boats; the yogi learns to use the mind’s energy.",
35:"Commentators note gṛhyate, “is held”: the mind can be grasped. Madhusūdana says practice and detachment together make it possible. Many find it helpful to see the two as two oars of a boat: both are needed to move straight.",
36:"Commentators note vaśyātmanā, “by one who is self-controlled”: discipline makes yoga attainable. Madhusūdana says the right means include study, practice and devotion. The verse is honest: yoga is hard but not out of reach.",
37:"Commentators note śraddhayopetaḥ, “endowed with faith”: Arjuna asks about the sincere who fail. Madhusūdana says the question is about one who started well but died before finishing. Many find this question deeply personal.",
38:"Commentators note vimūḍhaḥ brahmaṇaḥ pathi, “confused on the path of Brahman”: Arjuna fears the seeker may lose the way. Madhusūdana says the fear is natural for beginners. Kṛṣṇa’s answer will remove it completely.",
39:"Commentators note aśeṣataḥ, “completely”: Arjuna wants the doubt fully removed. Madhusūdana says only the Lord, who sees past and future, can answer. Arjuna’s trust shows how far he has come since chapter 1.",
40:"Commentators note kalyāṇa-kṛt, “doer of good”: anyone who sincerely tries. Madhusūdana says neither in this world nor the next is such a person lost. The verse has given courage to countless seekers who fear they are failing.",
41:"Commentators note śāśvatīḥ samāḥ, “countless years”: the seeker enjoys a long reward before rebirth. Madhusūdana says the good home gives a fresh start. The verse explains why some are born into supportive circumstances.",
42:"Commentators note durlabhataram, “very hard to gain”: a birth among the wise is rarer than a birth among the rich. Madhusūdana says wisdom is more valuable than wealth. Many saints were born into simple families rich in devotion.",
43:"Commentators note bhūyaḥ, “again”: the seeker strives again from where he left off. Madhusūdana says progress is never lost. Many find comfort in the idea that each life continues the work of the last.",
44:"Commentators note avaśo’pi, “even unwillingly”: past practice pulls one forward. Madhusūdana says this is why some people feel drawn to spiritual life from childhood without knowing why. The verse explains a common experience.",
45:"Commentators note prayatnāt, “with effort”: grace and effort work together. Madhusūdana says many lives of practice lead to the goal. The view is long and patient, which many find freeing.",
46:"Commentators note adhikaḥ, “greater”: the yogi surpasses ascetics, scholars and ritualists. Madhusūdana says the yogi unites what the others separate. Kṛṣṇa’s direct call, “be a yogi”, is one of the most personal in the Gītā.",
47:"Commentators note mad-gatenāntar-ātmanā, “with inner self gone to me”: the best yogi’s heart has gone to the Lord. Madhusūdana says this verse is the bridge to the second six chapters. Chapter 7 opens by continuing straight from this verse.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
